# J-Spread Project Rules — Cursor AI Coding Standards
# This file is read automatically by Cursor for all code in this project.

## Project Overview
This is J-Spread, a commercial financial spreading platform for bank lending.
Tech stack: React + TypeScript frontend, planned C# / ASP.NET Core backend.
Architecture doc: /docs/JSpread_Architecture_v4.md

## Commenting Standards — MANDATORY

### File Headers
Every .ts and .tsx file MUST begin with a block comment:
/**
 * [Filename] — [One-line purpose]
 * 
 * [2-3 sentences on what this file does and its role in the architecture.]
 * [Mention key dependencies or which components consume/produce its exports.]
 */

### Interfaces and Types
Every interface and type MUST have a JSDoc comment explaining the CONCEPT, not just restating the type name.
Every field that is not immediately self-evident MUST have an inline comment.
Financial domain fields (lineItemCode, tags, period types, statement quality, etc.) require
especially detailed comments explaining the banking/accounting meaning.

### Functions and Callbacks
Every exported function MUST have a JSDoc comment with:
- What it does
- Why it exists (business reason, not just technical)
- Any non-obvious behavior or side effects

### Business Logic
Any code implementing financial rules, reclassification logic, cash flow derivation,
combine logic, or analytical calculations MUST be heavily commented explaining the "why"
from a commercial banking perspective. A developer with no banking background should be
able to understand the intent from the comments alone.

## Code Style

### Architecture Principles
- The grid is an INPUT/OUTPUT interface, not the data model
- Real data is tagged values: each value knows WHAT it is (lineItemCode), WHEN it is (period), WHO it belongs to (entity)
- Analysis is driven by tags/queries, never by grid position
- Row position within sections determines classification for subtotal purposes
- Cash flow is DERIVED from BS + IS per entity; combined CF is derived from combined BS + IS
- Highlights, comments, and formatting are attributes of specific value cells (row × period × statement × entity)

### React Patterns
- Functional components with hooks only (no class components)
- State that will eventually come from backend should be structured to match expected API shape
- Keep FinancialGrid as a pure display/input component — it receives data via props
- Business logic belongs in hooks, utils, or App-level handlers, not in grid rendering

### TypeScript
- Strict typing everywhere — no `any` unless absolutely unavoidable with a comment explaining why
- Use discriminated unions for statement types, row types, etc.
- Prefer interfaces over type aliases for object shapes
- Export all types from types/ directory files

### Naming
- lineItemCode: dot-notation taxonomy (e.g., Asset.Current.Tangible.AR.Trade)
- periodId: system-generated unique identifier for a specific period instance
- entityId: system-generated unique identifier for an entity
- Statement types: 'BS' | 'IS' | 'CF'
- Row types: 'data' | 'subtotal' | 'total' | 'sectionHeader' | 'spacer'

### File Organization
- components/ — React UI components and their CSS
- types/ — All TypeScript interfaces, types, and type utilities
- data/ — Mock data and eventually API service files
- hooks/ — Custom React hooks
- utils/ — Pure utility functions (formatting, math, helpers)
